          "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Righteous flourishing: the palm tree most commonly represents the godly person’s uprightness, vitality, fruitfulness, and enduring prosperity under God’s care.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Psalm 92:12–15 explicitly compares the righteous to a flourishing palm tree and emphasizes continued fruitfulness in old age.\",\"Psalm 1:3 and Jeremiah 17:7–8, though describing trees more generally rather than palms, are often used as parallel imagery for the fruitful, enduring life of the righteous.\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Victory, triumph, and joyful acclaim, especially Christ’s kingship and the final victory of the redeemed.\",\"basis\":[\"John 12:12–13 describes the crowd greeting Jesus with palm branches at his triumphal entry.\",\"Revelation 7:9–10 depicts the redeemed standing before God and the Lamb with palm branches.\",\"Leviticus 23:40 associates palm branches with festal rejoicing before the Lord.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Beauty, stateliness, and desirability, particularly in bridal or nuptial imagery.\",\"basis\":[\"Song of Songs 7:7–8 compares the beloved’s stature to a palm tree and her breasts to its clusters.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Sacred life, paradisal abundance, or the beauty of God’s dwelling.\",\"basis\":[\"Palm-tree designs decorate Solomon’s temple in 1 Kings 6:29, 32, and 35.\",\"Palm trees also appear throughout Ezekiel’s visionary temple in Ezekiel 40:16–41:26.\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Rest, refreshment, and divine provision in a wilderness setting.\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 15:27 and Numbers 33:9 describe Israel camping at Elim, with its twelve springs and seventy palm trees.\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"notes\":\"There is no universally settled, one-to-one symbolism for tamar in every biblical context. Psalm 92 supplies the clearest explicit symbolic comparison and makes righteous flourishing the strongest meaning for the palm tree itself. Victory and triumph are also highly recognizable Christian associations, but they arise chiefly from palm branches in the New Testament and later Christian iconography.\"}"
        }
      ],
